      "source": "review",
      "review_round": 1,
      "summary": "The AC2 test checks arity at runtime with withCompletionDelivery.length === 3 but never shows that leaving out the root argument fails TypeScript checking. tsconfig.json includes no tests/** path, so neither `bun run typecheck` nor `bun test` type-checks tests/workflow-autocommit.test.ts. A regression to `root?: string` with `root ?? process.cwd()` in the body would keep arity 3, compile cleanly and pass both AC1 and AC2. Fix: add a compile-time check that a type-checking step actually runs, such as a @ts-expect-error call without root in a typechecked file, or a test that runs tsc on a fixture and expects the error.",
      "anchor": "sha256:f61da9d4be714e98b58483479487fcee88e1c0a8b230de216e6a4c5f36ba9ebd",
      "evidence": {
        "trigger": "Change the signature to `withCompletionDelivery<T>(taskId: string, result: T, root?: string)` and read evidence from `root ?? process.cwd()`. Function.length stays 3, so the new test passes. tsconfig.json only includes plugins/immune-brain/runtime/**, .pi-extension/**, skills/**, scripts/** and types/**, so typecheck still passes. A caller that leaves out root compiles and silently falls back to the server process cwd, which is the behavior BR-REQ-3 forbids, and no AC2 check fails.",
        "caller_chain": [
          "tests/workflow-autocommit.test.ts",
          "plugins/immune-brain/runtime/host-core/mcp_runtime.ts:withCompletionDelivery",
          "tsconfig.json#include"
        ],
        "violated": {
          "kind": "acceptance",
          "ref": "AC2"
        }
      },
